{:tier :glm-5.2, :verse 33, :comments ["<b>And when a man opens a pit.</b> It was covered and he uncovered it." "<b>Or when a man digs.</b> Why is this said? If for opening he is liable, for digging is it not all the more so? Rather, to bring one who digs after another who digs — he is liable (Bava Kamma 51)." "<b>And does not cover it.</b> This teaches: if he covered it, he is exempt. And Scripture speaks of one who digs in the public domain." "<b>An ox or a donkey.</b> The same applies to all domesticated and wild animals — for wherever 'ox or donkey' is stated, we learn that 'ox' from the Sabbath, as it says ״לְמַעַן יָנוּחַ שׁוֹרְךָ וַחֲמֹרֶךָ״ (Exodus 23): just as there, all domesticated and wild animals are like the ox — since it is stated elsewhere ״וְכָל בְּהֶמְתֶּךָ״ (Deuteronomy 5) — so too here, all domesticated and wild animals are like the ox. And 'ox' and 'donkey' are stated only to teach: ox and not man, donkey and not utensils (Bava Kamma 53)."], :book :exodus, :voice :rashi, :lang :en, :chapter 21, :notes {}, :source-witnesses nil, :timestamp "2026-08-02T08:53:46.429128930Z", :translated-from :he, :model "glm-5.2"}